Problem: karr-foundation resolves the agent command as CLI --command > config default_command > .karr command > claude: flag. With a global default_command in config.yml, EVERY discovered board becomes an agent board and a repo cannot opt out — a .karr file cannot override it, and .karr is local-machine state anyway, not board state.

Feature: a per-board disable flag stored in the karr git structure (refs/karr/config) so it syncs with the board like any other karr state, and every foundation instance on every machine respects it.

Suggested shape (implementer makes the final call):
- CLI: `karr disable [--reason "why"]` / `karr enable` (or `karr config set foundation.enabled false` — pick what fits the existing config command surface).
- Foundation checks the flag FIRST, before agent-command resolution and before the drain decision, and skips the board entirely.
- Precedence: disable wins over CLI --command, config default_command, .karr, and claude: true. Decide whether --force overrides it or an explicit per-repo override is required (leaning: --force does NOT override; disabled means disabled).
- `karr-foundation --status` overview shows the disabled state + reason.
- Tests alongside t/30-34 foundation tests.

Reference case: dbio-informix — abandoned driver (DBD::Informix needs the unobtainable Informix CSDK). Its board is a parked backlog with actionable-looking tasks (backlog + review) that foundation must never drain or auto-block. Disabling the board in its own karr state is the correct, syncing place for that fact.
